(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "7.2", title: "Computational thinking and flowcharts", q: "How do computer scientists solve problems before they write any code?", lessons: [
{ t: "What is an algorithm?", o: "I can explain what an algorithm is and write one as numbered steps.",
  k: ["algorithm: a set of step-by-step instructions that solves a problem", "sequence: doing steps in the correct order", "instruction: one single step to carry out"],
  d: ["Write the steps for brushing your teeth. How many did you need?", "Any ordered list. Most people miss steps such as taking the lid off the toothpaste."],
  s: ["Algorithms are everywhere|A recipe, directions to school, a dance routine|Each one is a list of steps in order|Follow the steps and the problem is solved",
      "The teacher-robot|A robot does exactly what it is told and nothing more|'Put jam on the bread' fails if the jar is still closed|Computers are the same: they cannot guess what you meant",
      "Writing an algorithm|Number each step|One action per step|Start each step with a verb: pick up, turn, press"],
  a: "In pairs, write an algorithm for making a jam sandwich. Test it on the teacher-robot, then rewrite it to fix every step that failed. Support: order the 10 jumbled step cards first. Stretch: write an algorithm that works for any sandwich filling.",
  w: [["What is an algorithm?", "A set of step-by-step instructions that solves a problem."], ["Give two examples of algorithms from everyday life.", "Any two, for example a recipe, directions, instructions for a game."], ["Why does the order of steps matter? Give an example.", "A different order gives a different result, for example putting on shoes before socks."], ["Rewrite as clear steps: 'Make some toast.'", "For example: 1 Take one slice of bread. 2 Put it in the toaster. 3 Push the lever down. 4 Wait until it pops up. 5 Take the toast out."], ["Explain why a computer needs more detailed instructions than a person.", "A person fills gaps using experience. A computer only does exactly what each instruction says."]],
  x: ["How many steps did your first sandwich algorithm have, and how many after fixing it?", "Two numbers, with the second larger."] },
{ t: "Precision and ambiguity", o: "I can spot unclear instructions and rewrite them precisely.",
  k: ["ambiguous: able to be understood in more than one way", "precise: exact, with only one meaning", "bug: a mistake in an algorithm or program"],
  d: ["'Walk to the door.' What information is missing?", "Which door, how far, which direction, when to stop."],
  s: ["Ambiguous instructions|'Turn' - which way? how far?|'Add some water' - how much?|People guess; computers cannot",
      "Making it precise|Give amounts: 'turn right 90 degrees'|Give units: 'walk forward 3 steps'|Give an end point: 'until you reach the wall'",
      "Bugs|A missing, wrong or misplaced step is a bug|Find bugs by following the steps exactly|Fixing bugs is called debugging"],
  a: "Rewrite the eight ambiguous instructions on the sheet so each has only one meaning. Then write precise directions from the classroom door to your seat, and let a partner follow them exactly. Support: choose the precise version from two options. Stretch: write directions that would still work if the furniture were moved.",
  w: [["What does ambiguous mean?", "It can be understood in more than one way."], ["Make precise: 'Turn and walk a bit.'", "For example: Turn left 90 degrees and walk forward 4 steps."], ["What is a bug?", "A mistake in an algorithm or program."], ["Find the bug: 1 Fill kettle. 2 Pour water into cup. 3 Switch kettle on. 4 Add teabag.", "Steps 2 and 3 are in the wrong order: the water is poured before it is boiled."], ["Explain why precise instructions matter more for a computer than for a friend.", "A friend can ask questions or use common sense. A computer follows the instruction exactly, even when it is wrong."]],
  x: ["Write one precise instruction that includes a number and a unit.", "For example: Walk forward 5 steps."] },
{ t: "Lab: draw my picture", o: "I can give and follow precise instructions and improve them after testing.",
  k: ["test: to try an algorithm to see whether it works", "debug: to find and fix mistakes", "refine: to improve something step by step"],
  d: ["How would you describe a square to someone who has never seen one?", "Four straight sides of equal length joined by four right angles."],
  s: ["The challenge|Sit back to back with a partner|One describes a picture; the other draws it|No looking, no questions",
      "What usually goes wrong|Size and position are left out|'Next to' and 'above' are unclear|Steps are given in a confusing order",
      "Test and refine|Compare the drawing with the original|Find the first instruction that went wrong|Rewrite it and try again"],
  a: "Take turns to describe and draw the shape pictures. After each round, list the instructions that caused errors and improve them. Record your best set of instructions. Support: use the picture made of three shapes. Stretch: describe a picture using only grid coordinates.",
  w: [["Why is testing an algorithm important?", "To find out whether it really works and where it goes wrong."], ["Give two details that make a drawing instruction precise.", "Any two: size, position, direction, shape name, order."], ["What does debug mean?", "To find and fix mistakes."], ["Improve: 'Draw a circle near the box.'", "For example: Draw a circle 2 cm wide, touching the top edge of the box in the centre."], ["Explain how using a grid with coordinates removes ambiguity.", "Each point has exactly one address, so position and size are stated with numbers and cannot be misunderstood."]],
  x: ["Which of your instructions caused the biggest error, and how did you fix it?", "A specific instruction and its improved version."] },
{ t: "Decomposition", o: "I can break a large problem into smaller parts.",
  k: ["decomposition: breaking a problem into smaller, easier parts", "sub-problem: one of the smaller parts", "computational thinking: solving problems in a way a computer could carry out"],
  d: ["You have to tidy a very messy bedroom. Where do you start?", "Split it up: clothes, books, rubbish, bed. Do one part at a time."],
  s: ["Big problems are hard|'Organise a school trip' is too big to do in one go|There are too many things to think about at once|Easy to miss something important",
      "Decomposition|Split the problem into parts: transport, cost, food, permission|Split each part again if it is still too big|Solve the small parts one at a time",
      "Why it helps|Each part is easier to solve|Different people can work on different parts|Parts can be tested separately"],
  a: "Decompose the problem 'Run a Year 7 sports day' into a tree diagram with at least three levels. Support: start from the four main branches provided. Stretch: mark which sub-problems depend on others and must be done first.",
  w: [["What is decomposition?", "Breaking a problem into smaller, easier parts."], ["Give three sub-problems of 'make a birthday party'.", "Any three, for example invitations, food, games, venue."], ["Give two advantages of decomposing a problem.", "Any two: easier to solve, can share the work, can test parts separately, less likely to forget something."], ["Decompose 'make a video game' into four parts.", "For example characters, controls, scoring, levels."], ["A sub-problem is still too difficult. What should you do, and when do you stop?", "Decompose it again. Stop when each part is small enough to solve directly."]],
  x: ["Name one large task from school that you could decompose.", "Any large task, for example a project or revising for a test."] },
{ t: "Pattern recognition", o: "I can find patterns and use them to solve problems faster.",
  k: ["pattern: something that repeats or is similar", "pattern recognition: spotting similarities between problems or within one problem", "rule: a statement that describes a pattern"],
  d: ["What comes next: 2, 4, 8, 16, ...? What is the rule?", "32. Each number is double the one before."],
  s: ["Spotting patterns|Number patterns: 3, 6, 9, 12|Shape patterns: square, circle, square, circle|Patterns in problems: drawing a square repeats 'forward, turn' four times",
      "Using patterns|Write the repeated part once|Say how many times to repeat it|The algorithm becomes shorter and easier to check",
      "Patterns between problems|Drawing a triangle is like drawing a square with different numbers|A solution to one problem can be reused for a similar one|This saves time"],
  a: "Complete the pattern puzzles, writing the rule for each. Then rewrite the 16-step 'draw a square' instructions using a repeat. Support: complete the first six puzzles. Stretch: write one set of instructions that draws any regular polygon.",
  w: [["What is pattern recognition?", "Spotting similarities or repeats in or between problems."], ["Find the rule: 5, 10, 15, 20.", "Add 5 each time (multiples of 5)."], ["Shorten: forward, turn, forward, turn, forward, turn, forward, turn.", "Repeat 4 times: forward, turn."], ["How is drawing a hexagon similar to drawing a square?", "Both repeat 'forward, turn'. Only the number of repeats and the angle change."], ["Explain why finding a pattern makes an algorithm easier to fix.", "The repeated part is written once, so a correction is made in one place."]],
  x: ["Give one pattern you noticed today and its rule.", "Any pattern with a correct rule."] },
{ t: "Lab: abstraction with a metro-style map", o: "I can remove unnecessary detail to make a useful model.",
  k: ["abstraction: removing detail that is not needed to solve the problem", "model: a simplified version of something real", "detail: a small piece of information"],
  d: ["A metro map does not show streets or buildings. Why is it still useful?", "It shows only what a traveller needs: stations, lines and connections."],
  s: ["Abstraction|Keep what matters for the problem|Leave out what does not|What matters depends on who will use it",
      "The metro map|Keeps: stations, lines, order, interchanges|Removes: real distances, streets, bends|Easier to read than a real map",
      "Your task|Make a metro-style map of the school|Decide what a new student needs to know|Rooms become stations; corridors become lines"],
  a: "Draw a metro-style map of the school showing key rooms as stations and routes as coloured lines. List three details you chose to leave out and why. Support: use the list of 12 rooms to include. Stretch: make a second version for a different user, such as a delivery driver.",
  w: [["What is abstraction?", "Removing detail that is not needed to solve the problem."], ["Name two things a metro map keeps and two it removes.", "Keeps: stations, lines or connections. Removes: real distances, streets, buildings."], ["Why is a simplified model sometimes better than the real thing?", "It is easier to understand and shows only what is needed."], ["You are designing a weather app icon for rain. What detail would you keep and what would you remove?", "Keep a cloud and drops. Remove realistic shading, exact cloud shape and background."], ["Explain why two people might make different abstractions of the same school.", "They have different users or purposes, so different details matter."]],
  x: ["Name the four parts of computational thinking met so far.", "Algorithms, decomposition, pattern recognition, abstraction."] },
{ t: "Flowchart symbols and sequence", o: "I can draw a flowchart for a sequence using the correct symbols.",
  k: ["flowchart: a diagram that shows the steps of an algorithm", "terminator: the rounded symbol for Start and Stop", "process: the rectangle symbol for an action"],
  d: ["Why might a diagram be easier to follow than a paragraph of instructions?", "You can see the order and the routes at a glance."],
  s: ["The symbols|Rounded box: Start or Stop|Rectangle: a process, something that is done|Parallelogram: input or output|Arrows show the order",
      "Sequence in a flowchart|One Start at the top, one Stop at the bottom|Each box has one arrow in and one arrow out|Read from top to bottom",
      "Example: add two numbers|Start|Input A, Input B (parallelograms)|Total = A + B (rectangle)|Output Total, then Stop"],
  a: "Draw flowcharts for three sequences: making a cup of tea, calculating the area of a rectangle, and converting pounds to pence. Label every symbol type once. Support: cut and arrange the pre-drawn symbols. Stretch: draw a flowchart that inputs three test marks and outputs the average.",
  w: [["Which symbol is used for Start and Stop?", "A rounded box (terminator)."], ["Which symbol is used for input and output?", "A parallelogram."], ["What do the arrows show?", "The order in which the steps are carried out."], ["Write the four boxes, in order, for a flowchart that inputs a length in metres and outputs it in centimetres.", "Start; Input Metres; Centimetres = Metres x 100; Output Centimetres; Stop."], ["A flowchart has two Start symbols. Explain the problem.", "It is unclear where to begin, so the algorithm is ambiguous. A flowchart needs exactly one Start."]],
  x: ["Sketch the symbol for a process and name one example of a process.", "A rectangle, for example Total = A + B."] },
{ t: "Selection: decisions in flowcharts", o: "I can use a decision symbol with yes and no branches.",
  k: ["selection: choosing which steps to do, based on a condition", "decision: the diamond symbol that asks a yes or no question", "condition: a test that is either true or false"],
  d: ["'If it is raining, take an umbrella.' What is the question being asked?", "Is it raining? Yes or no."],
  s: ["Selection|Sometimes the algorithm must choose|The choice depends on a condition|A condition is always true or false",
      "The decision symbol|A diamond with a question inside: Age >= 12?|One arrow in, two arrows out|Label the arrows Yes and No",
      "Example: pass or fail|Input Mark|Mark >= 50? Yes: output 'Pass'. No: output 'Fail'|Both branches join again before Stop"],
  a: "Draw flowcharts with one decision for: checking whether a number is positive, deciding whether a pupil can go on a ride (height at least 120 cm), and applying a discount for spending over 50. Support: complete the three part-drawn flowcharts. Stretch: add a second decision to give three outcomes.",
  w: [["What shape is the decision symbol?", "A diamond."], ["How many arrows leave a decision symbol, and what are they labelled?", "Two, labelled Yes and No (or True and False)."], ["Write a condition to test whether Temperature is below 0.", "Temperature < 0?"], ["A flowchart inputs Age. If Age is 18 or more it outputs 'Adult', otherwise 'Child'. Write the condition and both outputs.", "Age >= 18? Yes: Output 'Adult'. No: Output 'Child'."], ["A student draws a diamond with only one arrow leaving it. Explain what is wrong.", "A decision must have two exits. With one, there is no alternative route, so it is not a decision."]],
  x: ["Write one condition from your own life as a yes or no question.", "Any true or false test, for example Homework finished?"] },
{ t: "Lab: flowcharts for everyday decisions", o: "I can design and test flowcharts that include decisions.",
  k: ["branch: one of the routes leaving a decision", "test data: values used to check an algorithm", "outcome: the result the algorithm produces"],
  d: ["A flowchart tests Mark >= 50. Which branch is followed for a mark of exactly 50?", "Yes, because 50 is greater than or equal to 50."],
  s: ["Plan first|What is the input?|What question decides the route?|What is the output on each branch?",
      "Draw|Start, input, decision, two outputs, Stop|Label Yes and No clearly|Keep arrows from crossing",
      "Test|Choose a value for each branch|Choose the value exactly on the boundary|Follow the arrows with your finger and check the outcome"],
  a: "Using flowchart software or paper, design flowcharts for: a cinema ticket price by age, a traffic light crossing decision, and a login check. Test each with three values and record the outcomes. Support: test the supplied flowcharts and fix the one with a bug. Stretch: design a flowchart with two nested decisions for a grade (A, B or C).",
  w: [["What is test data?", "Values used to check that an algorithm works."], ["A decision is Age < 12. Which branch does Age = 12 follow?", "No."], ["Give three test values for the condition Height >= 120 and say why you chose each.", "For example 150 (yes branch), 100 (no branch), 120 (the boundary)."], ["Why must both branches lead to Stop eventually?", "So the algorithm always finishes whichever route is taken."], ["Explain why the boundary value is the most important test.", "Mistakes such as using > when >= was needed only show up at the boundary."]],
  x: ["Which of your flowcharts had a bug, and how did testing reveal it?", "A specific bug found by a test value."] },
{ t: "Iteration: loops in flowcharts", o: "I can use a loop in a flowchart to repeat steps.",
  k: ["iteration: repeating steps", "loop: a route in a flowchart that goes back to an earlier step", "infinite loop: a loop that never ends"],
  d: ["Write the steps to clap 10 times without writing 'clap' ten times.", "Repeat 10 times: clap."],
  s: ["Iteration|Repeating steps saves writing them again|The loop continues while a condition is true, or until it becomes true|Every loop needs a way to stop",
      "Loops in a flowchart|A decision controls the loop|One branch goes back up to repeat|The other branch carries on",
      "Example: keep asking|Input Password|Password correct? No: go back to Input. Yes: continue|Without a way to become Yes, this is an infinite loop"],
  a: "Draw flowcharts with a loop for: asking for a number until it is between 1 and 10, washing dishes until the pile is empty, and a countdown from 10 to 1. Support: add the missing loop arrow to the part-drawn flowcharts. Stretch: draw a flowchart that allows only three password attempts.",
  w: [["What is iteration?", "Repeating steps."], ["Which symbol controls whether a loop repeats?", "A decision (diamond)."], ["What is an infinite loop?", "A loop whose condition never allows it to stop."], ["Describe in words a flowchart that keeps asking for a mark until it is 100 or less.", "Input Mark; Mark <= 100? No: go back to Input Mark. Yes: continue to the next step."], ["A loop says: Count = 1; repeat while Count < 5: output Count. Explain why it never ends and how to fix it.", "Count never changes, so it is always less than 5. Add Count = Count + 1 inside the loop."]],
  x: ["Name the three building blocks of every algorithm.", "Sequence, selection, iteration."] },
{ t: "Variables and counting in flowcharts", o: "I can use a variable to count or keep a total in a flowchart.",
  k: ["variable: a named place that stores a value which can change", "assignment: giving a variable a value", "counter: a variable that goes up by one each time"],
  d: ["A scoreboard shows 3. A goal is scored. What happens to the number, and where was the old number kept?", "It becomes 4. The scoreboard stored the value so it could be changed."],
  s: ["Variables|A variable has a name and a value|The value can change while the algorithm runs|Example: Score, Lives, Total",
      "Assignment|Count = 0 sets a starting value|Count = Count + 1 means: take the old value, add 1, store the result|Read = as 'becomes'",
      "Counting and totalling|Set the variable to 0 before the loop|Change it inside the loop|Output it after the loop"],
  a: "Draw flowcharts that: count from 1 to 10, total five numbers typed in, and count how many of ten marks are over 50. Support: fill in the missing assignment boxes. Stretch: draw a flowchart that outputs the average of numbers entered until 0 is typed.",
  w: [["What is a variable?", "A named place that stores a value which can change."], ["Count holds 6. What does it hold after Count = Count + 1?", "7."], ["Why is Total set to 0 before a loop starts?", "So the total starts from nothing and old or unknown values are not included."], ["Total = 0. The numbers 4, 7 and 2 are added one at a time. Write the value of Total after each.", "4, 11, 13."], ["Explain the difference between a counter and a running total.", "A counter goes up by 1 each time something happens. A running total goes up by the value of each item."]],
  x: ["Write an assignment that adds 5 to a variable called Points.", "Points = Points + 5"] },
{ t: "Lab: flowchart for a guessing game", o: "I can combine sequence, selection, iteration and variables in one flowchart.",
  k: ["secret number: the value the player must guess", "attempt: one try at guessing", "feedback: a message that tells the player how close they are"],
  d: ["I am thinking of a number from 1 to 100. Your guess is 50 and I say 'too high'. What should you guess next, and why?", "25, the middle of what is left, to remove half the possibilities."],
  s: ["The game|The computer holds a secret number|The player guesses|The computer says too high, too low or correct",
      "What the flowchart needs|Variables: Secret, Guess, Attempts|A loop that repeats until Guess = Secret|Decisions for too high and too low",
      "Build it in stages|First: one guess, correct or wrong|Next: add the loop|Then: add too high and too low, and count attempts"],
  a: "Design the guessing game flowchart in three stages, testing after each stage with Secret = 37. Record the route for the guesses 50, 25, 37. Support: build stage 1 and stage 2 only. Stretch: end the game after seven attempts with a 'you lose' message.",
  w: [["Name three variables the guessing game needs.", "Secret, Guess, Attempts (or similar)."], ["Which condition ends the loop?", "Guess = Secret."], ["Secret is 37 and Guess is 50. What message is output?", "Too high."], ["Where should Attempts = Attempts + 1 be placed?", "Inside the loop, once for each guess."], ["Explain why building the flowchart in stages is better than drawing it all at once.", "Each stage can be tested, so a bug is found in the small part just added. This is decomposition."]],
  x: ["How many attempts did your flowchart count for the guesses 50, 25, 37?", "3."] },
{ t: "Trace tables", o: "I can trace an algorithm and record how its variables change.",
  k: ["trace table: a table that records the value of each variable as an algorithm runs", "dry run: following an algorithm by hand", "output: what the algorithm displays"],
  d: ["X = 2. Then X = X + 3. Then X = X x 2. What is X?", "10."],
  s: ["Why trace?|To check an algorithm without a computer|To find exactly where a bug happens|It is a skill tested in every exam",
      "How to trace|One column per variable, plus one for output|Follow the flowchart one box at a time|Write a new value only when a variable changes",
      "Example|Count = 1; while Count <= 3: output Count, Count = Count + 1|Count column: 1, 2, 3, 4|Output column: 1, 2, 3"],
  a: "Complete trace tables for the four flowcharts on the sheet, then state what each algorithm does. Support: the first two tables have the first rows filled in. Stretch: trace the flowchart with two variables and a total, then change one box and predict the new output.",
  w: [["What is a trace table for?", "Recording how the variables change as an algorithm runs, to check it or find bugs."], ["What is a dry run?", "Following an algorithm by hand without a computer."], ["Trace: Total = 0; add 5; add 5; add 5. Write the values of Total.", "0, 5, 10, 15."], ["Trace: N = 10; while N > 0: N = N - 4. Write every value of N.", "10, 6, 2, -2."], ["A loop is: Count = 1; while Count <= 3. Explain why the Count column ends with 4.", "Count becomes 4 on the last pass, the condition 4 <= 3 is false, and the loop stops."]],
  x: ["When do you write a new row or value in a trace table?", "When a variable changes or something is output."] },
{ t: "Debugging faulty flowcharts", o: "I can find and fix errors in a flowchart.",
  k: ["logic error: the algorithm runs but gives the wrong result", "expected result: what the algorithm should produce", "actual result: what the algorithm does produce"],
  d: ["A flowchart should output the larger of two numbers, but for 3 and 8 it outputs 3. What kind of mistake might cause this?", "The decision is the wrong way round, or the outputs are on the wrong branches."],
  s: ["Common flowchart bugs|Yes and No labels swapped|Wrong comparison: > when >= was needed|Variable never changed, so the loop never ends|Starting value missing",
      "A method for debugging|Decide the expected result for a test value|Trace to find the actual result|The first row where they differ shows the faulty box",
      "Fix one thing|Change one box only|Trace again with the same value|Then test a boundary value"],
  a: "Each of the five flowcharts contains one bug. For each: write the expected result, trace to find the actual result, circle the faulty box and write the correction. Support: the type of bug is named for the first three. Stretch: one flowchart has two bugs. Find both.",
  w: [["What is a logic error?", "The algorithm runs but produces the wrong result."], ["A flowchart should output 'Pass' for 50 or more but uses Mark > 50. Which mark shows the bug?", "50."], ["Name two common flowchart bugs.", "Any two: swapped Yes and No, wrong comparison, missing starting value, variable not updated."], ["Expected output is 1, 2, 3 but actual is 1, 2. The loop condition is Count < 3. Give the fix.", "Change it to Count <= 3 (or Count < 4)."], ["Explain why you should retest with the same data after fixing a bug.", "To confirm the fix works and has not broken something else."]],
  x: ["What is the difference between the expected and the actual result?", "Expected is what it should do. Actual is what it does."] },
{ t: "Lab: swap, trace and fix", o: "I can test a partner's flowchart and give useful feedback.",
  k: ["peer review: checking someone else's work and giving feedback", "boundary value: a value at the edge of a condition", "correction: a change that fixes an error"],
  d: ["Why is it easier to find mistakes in someone else's work than in your own?", "You follow what is written, not what you meant to write."],
  s: ["Your flowchart|Draw a flowchart for the problem card you are given|Include at least one decision and one loop|Do not tell your partner what it should output",
      "Review|Trace your partner's flowchart with three test values|Include a boundary value|Record expected and actual results",
      "Feedback|Say which box is wrong and why|Suggest the correction|Say one thing that is done well"],
  a: "Draw a flowchart for your problem card, swap with a partner, and trace theirs with three test values. Write feedback, swap back and fix your own. Support: use a problem card with one decision only. Stretch: deliberately hide one subtle bug for your partner to find.",
  w: [["What is peer review?", "Checking someone else's work and giving feedback."], ["How many test values should you use, and what kinds?", "At least three: one for each branch and a boundary value."], ["Write one piece of useful feedback about a flowchart.", "It names the box, says what is wrong and suggests a fix, for example: the decision should be Age >= 12, not Age > 12."], ["Your partner says your loop never ends. What should you check first?", "Whether the variable in the loop condition changes inside the loop."], ["Explain why 'It doesn't work' is not useful feedback.", "It does not say where the problem is or how to fix it."]],
  x: ["What was the most useful feedback you received?", "A specific comment."] },
{ t: "Revision carousel", o: "I can recall and apply the key ideas of computational thinking.",
  k: ["revise: to go over learning again so it is remembered", "recall: to bring something back from memory", "apply: to use knowledge on a new problem"],
  d: ["Without looking at your notes, list as many flowchart symbols as you can.", "Terminator, process, input/output, decision, arrow."],
  s: ["Four parts of computational thinking|Decomposition: break it down|Pattern recognition: spot repeats|Abstraction: remove detail|Algorithm design: write the steps",
      "Three building blocks|Sequence: steps in order|Selection: a decision chooses the route|Iteration: a loop repeats steps",
      "Exam skills|Draw with the correct symbols|Trace with a table|Test with a boundary value"],
  a: "Rotate around six stations, eight minutes each: symbols match, decompose a problem, draw from a description, trace table, find the bug, write test data. Record a score at each. Support: take the hint card at each station. Stretch: at each station, write one extra question for the next group.",
  w: [["Match: breaking a problem into parts; removing unneeded detail; spotting repeats.", "Decomposition; abstraction; pattern recognition."], ["Name the three building blocks of algorithms.", "Sequence, selection, iteration."], ["Which symbol would you use for 'Output Total'?", "A parallelogram."], ["Trace: A = 3; B = A + 2; A = A x B. Give the final values.", "A = 15, B = 5."], ["Write three test values for the decision Speed > 30 and the expected branch for each.", "For example 40 Yes, 20 No, 30 No (boundary)."]],
  x: ["Which station did you find hardest? That is your revision target.", "A named station."] },
{ t: "Assessed task: design and trace an algorithm", o: "I can design an algorithm as a flowchart and prove it works with a trace table.",
  k: ["design: a plan for a solution", "evidence: proof that something works", "specification: a description of what the solution must do"],
  d: ["What three things must you check before handing in a flowchart?", "Correct symbols, labelled branches, and that it has been tested."],
  s: ["The task|Choose: a vending machine or a lift controller|Draw the flowchart with at least one decision, one loop and one variable|Work on your own",
      "Trace it|Use the test data provided|Complete the trace table|State the output",
      "Success criteria|Correct symbols and labelled arrows|The algorithm does what the specification says|The trace table matches the flowchart"],
  a: "Design your flowchart for the vending machine or lift, then complete the trace table for the given test data. Support: use the specification with numbered requirements to tick off. Stretch: add a second feature, such as giving change, and trace it.",
  w: [["List the inputs your algorithm needs.", "For example coin value and item choice, or current floor and requested floor."], ["Write the condition used in your main decision.", "For example Money >= Price? or Floor < Target?"], ["Name the variable that changes inside your loop.", "For example Money or Floor."], ["Give the output for the first set of test data.", "The value from the student's own trace."], ["Explain one improvement you would make with more time.", "A specific, relevant feature or fix."]],
  x: ["Does your trace table match your flowchart? How do you know?", "Yes, with a reason such as each row follows one box."] },
{ t: "Feedback and reflection", o: "I can use feedback to improve my algorithm and reflect on my learning.",
  k: ["feedback: comments that help you improve", "improve: to make better", "target: a specific thing to work on next"],
  d: ["Which part of computational thinking do you use most without noticing?", "Any, with an example, such as decomposition when planning homework."],
  s: ["Read your feedback|Find your strength|Find your next step|Check your level for each outcome",
      "Improve|Correct the box or row your teacher marked|Retrace with the same test data|Show the change in a different colour",
      "Reflect|What can I do now that I could not do before?|Which mistake taught me the most?|What question do I still have?"],
  a: "Correct your assessed flowchart and trace table using the feedback, then complete the unit reflection. Support: your teacher has marked the exact box to fix. Stretch: explain how your flowchart could be turned into a Scratch program next unit.",
